<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mfecane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1810–<a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mfecane was a period of widespread warfare, migration and state formation in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the early nineteenth century, associated with the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> kingdom under Shaka. It sent waves of armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> groups northwards across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>. Their incursions in the 1820s and 1830s contributed to the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mfecane.jpg" alt="Mfecane" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Departure_of_the_Fingoes-1840.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Mfecane was a period of heightened military conflict and migration in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, tied to state formation and expansion. It resulted from the interplay of pre-existing political centralisation with the effects of international trade, environmental instability and European colonisation. State formation had been intensifying since at least the late 1700s and accelerated once the international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade opened, since the trade let leaders amass wealth to build instruments of control.</p><p>Deep drought struck south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the early 19th century, aggravated by the atmospheric effects of volcanic eruptions in 1809 and 1815. Unequal distribution of land and food stores meant ordinary people suffered famine, while leaders facing falling production and scarcer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> turned to raiding and conquest. A second stage in the 1820s and 1830s was driven largely by slave and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raiding by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a>, Basters and other groups armed and mounted by European settlers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the east the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> and Mthethwa paramountcies expanded in the 1810s. The Ndwandwe defeated and killed the Mthethwa leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a>, and their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide kaLanga</a> then fought Shaka kaSenzangakhona's amaZulu. In 1826 Shaka put the Ndwandwe under Sikhunyana to flight at the Izindololwane Hills, and the Ndwandwe state collapsed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Kingdom</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a> led his followers, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a>, north of the Zambezi River. Shaka was assassinated in September 1828 by his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mhlangana">Mhlangana</a>.</p><p>In the interior, amaHlubi under Mpangazita attacked Queen MmaNthatisi's Batlôkwa in 1822, and in 1824 the Sotho polities began coalescing under Moshoeshoe. Mzilikazi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> raided widely and, after the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> settlers and the battles of Vegtkop and Mosega in 1837 and 1838, were driven north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> to Matabeleland, where Mzilikazi set up his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Traditional estimates put the death toll at 1 million to 2 million, but these figures are controversial, and some recent scholars revise them significantly downwards. The period saw the formation of new states, institutions and ethnic identities, among them the kingdom that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a> under Moshoeshoe. The label first emerged in the 1830s and blamed Shaka, who was alleged to have waged near-genocidal wars; since the latter half of the 20th century scholars have largely rejected this for lack of evidence.</p><p>In 1988 Julian Cobbing argued that the roots lay in the labour needs of Portuguese slave traders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a> and of settlers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. Elizabeth Eldredge challenged him, and by the early 2000s a consensus recognised many causes before and after Shaka came to power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mfecane” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s weakness was exposed around 1840, when the Kololo, a Sotho-speaking people led by Sebitwane who had migrated north during the upheavals of the <b>Mfecane</b>, defeated the Lozi, then divided by a succession war after Mulambwa’s death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They were unfamiliar with the regimental organisation and close-combat stabbing-spear tactics of the Nguni forces displaced by the <b>Mfecane</b>, and the loosely organised Rozvi levies could not stand against them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Shaka the army raided far beyond the kingdom, and his campaigns, together with drought and the slave trade from Delagoa Bay, are associated with the wider upheavals known as the <b>Mfecane</b>, a term whose interpretation is debated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>As the wars of the <b>Lifaqane</b> scattered communities across the highveld, he welcomed refugees from many groups, lending them cattle and building a kingdom from them.</blockquote><a href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Basotho Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
